### 14\. Questions for deep Bible study and group discussion

* What is the difference between authority (exousia) and power (dynamis)?

* Why did demons recognize Jesus even when some religious leaders rejected Him?

* Why did Jesus command demons to be silent?

* What does the encounter with Legion reveal about the relationship between Christ's authority and human freedom?

* What did Jesus mean by binding the strong man?

* How does Colossians 2:13–15 connect forgiveness of sins with the defeat of spiritual powers?

* Why did the seventy disciples return rejoicing over their authority, and why did Jesus redirect their joy?

* What is the difference between using the name of Jesus as a formula and genuinely representing His authority?

* How does the believer's identity in Christ affect the way spiritual warfare should be approached?

* Why is the restoration of the human person more important than the spectacle of deliverance?

### 15\. Final conclusion: The supremacy of Christ

![Sherehe ya Yesu Kristo Mfalme:Ufalme wake si wa Dunia hii,bali ni wa Mbinguni! - | Vatican News](https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/ni6BADRpezTkruOWBnIc1h5hqWG1i_4o5jptljvCseFboKEJl6EW5t28Y5X3sdAf91EdEW2xukv8a1c85b07S5tqPlVyKA-6HhyQ6Rqv-nYk6I61GunVb-U7ODbJlNsT8ANTqEEBGkuphOTQlBAT9dXrExk7E0akN_xkvBJ-M59C1p4yxx4n1W34QIJ_ObhU?purpose=fullsize)

The entire biblical narrative establishes one central truth:

Jesus Christ is Lord.

He is Lord over creation.

He is Lord over the visible and invisible realms.

He is Lord over principalities and powers.

He is Lord over demons and unclean spirits.

He is Lord over sin and death.

And He is Lord over all things.

Demons may resist, deceive, tempt, accuse and oppress, but they cannot overthrow the sovereignty of God.

The ministry of Christ reveals that humanity's freedom is not found in becoming fascinated with the powers of darkness. It is found in coming under the Lordship of Jesus Christ.

The objective of the Gospel is not merely to make people capable of confronting demons.

It is to reconcile humanity to God, restore the image of God in human life, establish the reign of Christ in the heart, and bring creation under the purposes of its rightful Lord.

The ultimate revelation of Divine Authority is not the existence of an enemy who must be feared. It is the supremacy of a King whose authority cannot be overthrown.

"For he must reign, till he hath put all enemies under his feet." — 1 Corinthians 15:25 (KJV).

