### Further academic and biblical study resources

For the original-language and historical dimensions, these are useful starting points:

* [Oxford Bibliographies – Exorcism](https://academic.oup.com/reference/62341/reference-article-abstract/554111597?utm_source=chatgpt.com) — Scholarly overview of exorcism in biblical studies.

* [Exousia and Conflict in the Gospel of Mark](https://www.mdpi.com/2077-1444/14/10/1318?utm_source=chatgpt.com) — Academic exploration of authority in Mark's Gospel.

* [LARES – Daimonion](https://lares-lexicon.unibo.it/en/documents/lexicon_daimonion.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com) — Historical and Septuagint usage of the word for demons.

* [Jesus and Non-Levitical Impurity](https://scholar.csl.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1081&context=phd&utm_source=chatgpt.com) — Research on Mark 1 and Jesus' authority over unclean spirits.

* [Strong's Greek Dictionary](https://dl.bibleonline.ru/library/james-strong/StrongGreekDictionary.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com) — Original-language lexical reference.

My recommendation: Develop this as a foundational Christology and Kingdom Authority course, rather than simply a demonology course. The distinction matters: demonology studies the enemy, but Christology establishes the King. The King must remain the centre of the teaching.
